1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,176
This session follows Seven E sections five and six. The central
question is what changed for this person and what that change means
now.

2
00:00:10,176 --> 00:00:20,808
Older age can alter reserve and presentation, but it does not make
every patient frail, confused, dependent or incapable of deciding
about care.

3
00:00:20,808 --> 00:00:31,728
We will connect physiology and medicines to observations, then make
communication and movement safer. A category name should improve care
rather than replace assessment.

4
00:00:31,728 --> 00:00:40,032
An original evolving case will test the reasoning. It is not a school
assessment answer or a claim that reading certifies a handling skill.

5
00:00:40,032 --> 00:00:51,984
Ask the patient what they normally do and what feels different today.
Useful function includes walking to the kitchen, preparing food,
managing medicines or following a conversation.

6
00:00:51,984 --> 00:01:03,216
Ask a reliable witness when the last usual interaction occurred. A
vague statement that the patient is often confused does not explain a
new loss of attention or mobility.

7
00:01:03,216 --> 00:01:14,208
Distinguish chronic limitations from acute changes without dismissing
either. A person with dementia can develop delirium, stroke, injury or
infection.

8
00:01:14,208 --> 00:01:24,888
Record who supplied the baseline and what remains uncertain. A later
handover is safer when the next team can judge the reliability of the
comparison.

9
00:01:24,888 --> 00:01:36,120
Physiological reserve is the ability to respond to additional demand.
Someone can meet daily needs and then deteriorate when illness,
dehydration or injury adds stress.

10
00:01:36,120 --> 00:01:44,496
Age-related changes and chronic disease may reduce that margin, but
the amount varies substantially between people and organ systems.

11
00:01:44,496 --> 00:01:54,168
Do not learn a universal linear rate of aging or assume every organ
fails to the same degree. Establish the actual function and current
signs.

12
00:01:54,168 --> 00:02:05,616
The practical implication is a low threshold to investigate meaningful
new change, not permission to call all abnormalities normal aging or
to treat every older person identically.

13
00:02:05,616 --> 00:02:16,872
Vascular stiffness and changes in cardiac filling or conduction can
influence the response to illness. Chronic disease and medicines may
contribute as much as age itself.

14
00:02:16,872 --> 00:02:25,944
A normal-range pulse does not exclude blood loss or infection,
especially with a rate-limiting medicine. Assess the whole perfusion
picture.

15
00:02:25,944 --> 00:02:39,696
Compare pressure with a reliable baseline and current symptoms. A
relative fall may be concerning, but one systolic value below one
hundred twenty does not automatically prove hypovolemia or require
fluid.

16
00:02:39,696 --> 00:02:51,960
Likewise, hypertension is not harmless because it is common in older
adults. New neurological, chest or breathing findings still require
assessment through the appropriate pathway.

17
00:02:51,960 --> 00:03:01,296
Chest-wall changes, muscle weakness and chronic lung disease may
reduce breathing reserve. Cough and clearance can also be impaired.

18
00:03:01,296 --> 00:03:12,432
New tachypnea may be important even without fever or a dramatic
complaint. Assess depth, effort, air movement and mental response as
well as saturation.

19
00:03:12,432 --> 00:03:24,552
Do not apply a textbook age formula as proof that a low oxygen value
is acceptable. Use the actual clinical oxygen pathway and identify any
established chronic target.

20
00:03:24,552 --> 00:03:36,384
A first crackle or chronic edema does not alone establish heart
failure, but neither should new findings be dismissed. Use history,
distribution, trend and the full assessment.

21
00:03:36,384 --> 00:03:46,776
Some people need longer to hear, process or answer a question. Provide
support and time before deciding that the response represents
cognitive failure.

22
00:03:46,776 --> 00:03:58,752
Acute confusion, new focal weakness or a major functional decline is
not explained by getting older. Check breathing, glucose, perfusion
and relevant neurological findings.

23
00:03:58,752 --> 00:04:12,072
After a fall, head injury may be subtle or have delayed symptoms,
especially with anticoagulant use. Absence of severe headache or a
remembered head strike cannot independently clear it.

24
00:04:12,072 --> 00:04:22,800
Age-associated anatomy may help explain risk but does not guarantee a
particular bleed or outcome. Preserve alternatives while prioritizing
urgent assessment.

25
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:35,640
Kidney and liver function influence drug handling, and acute illness
may change clearance. Do not assume every older adult has identical
impairment or that an old normal result proves current safety.

26
00:04:35,640 --> 00:04:47,952
Reduced intake, vomiting, diarrhea or diuretic effects can reduce
circulating volume. Heart and kidney disease may simultaneously
increase the risk of fluid overload.

27
00:04:47,952 --> 00:04:59,784
Use perfusion, breathing, urine history and response to guide the real
care plan. Dry mouth or skin tenting alone is insufficient to diagnose
dehydration in older skin.

28
00:04:59,784 --> 00:05:09,744
This is not an automatic fluid, potassium or dose-adjustment
instruction. Use the permitted pathway and clinical consultation for
the actual patient.

29
00:05:09,744 --> 00:05:22,416
Skin may be more vulnerable to shear or adhesive injury, and some
people have fragile bones or painful joints. Ask about previous
handling difficulties and look at the actual tissue.

30
00:05:22,416 --> 00:05:32,496
Use appropriate padding and aids, and remove necessary adhesive
gently. Do not drag a patient across a surface or use their arms as
lifting handles.

31
00:05:32,496 --> 00:05:42,336
Expose only what is needed for an adequate assessment and cover again.
Warmth and privacy are active parts of care, not optional finishing
touches.

32
00:05:42,336 --> 00:05:52,344
Reassess after the move for pain, skin injury, breathing and device
position. A technically completed transfer is not proof that it was
harmless.

33
00:05:52,344 --> 00:06:02,928
An acute coronary event may occur without classic chest pain. New
unexplained weakness, dyspnea, syncope or nausea can be part of the
presentation.

34
00:06:02,928 --> 00:06:14,136
Ask what happened today that prompted help and how activity differs
from usual. Do not assume the patient would necessarily describe a
dangerous symptom using textbook words.

35
00:06:14,136 --> 00:06:25,392
Perform appropriate assessment, including an ECG when indicated,
without delaying primary care. The absence of pain does not remove the
need for the cardiac differential.

36
00:06:25,392 --> 00:06:36,024
Other causes of weakness remain possible. Atypical presentation should
widen useful assessment rather than force every older person into an
infarction diagnosis.

37
00:06:36,024 --> 00:06:49,200
Infection can present with a muted temperature response or new
functional loss. Consider it when an older person becomes acutely
unwell, but do not let it hide stroke, injury or medicine effects.

38
00:06:49,200 --> 00:07:02,064
Assess new respiratory symptoms, wounds, urinary-localizing symptoms
and other relevant sources. A source search should be focused and
respectful rather than indiscriminate exposure.

39
00:07:02,064 --> 00:07:13,008
Bacteriuria or odor alone does not prove a symptomatic urinary
infection. Delirium without local urinary or systemic signs needs a
broader cause assessment.

40
00:07:13,008 --> 00:07:25,560
Do not wait for hypotension, high fever or a positive laboratory test
to recognize credible deterioration. Use the actual local sepsis
pathway and ongoing clinical picture.

41
00:07:25,560 --> 00:07:37,248
Polypharmacy means multiple concurrent medicines. The count alone does
not prove inappropriate treatment, but it increases the need for
accurate history and interaction awareness.

42
00:07:37,248 --> 00:07:48,048
Medicines can cause symptoms, worsen another illness or hide a usual
response. Consider all three rather than naming an adverse effect and
stopping assessment.

43
00:07:48,048 --> 00:07:59,232
Ask about new prescriptions, dose changes, missed or repeated doses
and nonprescription products. A blister pack does not prove which dose
was actually taken.

44
00:07:59,232 --> 00:08:10,776
Report the evidence and seek the appropriate plan. This lecture does
not authorize stopping chronic treatment, reversing an anticoagulant
or changing another clinician's regimen.

45
00:08:10,776 --> 00:08:20,616
Beta blockers and some other medicines can limit heart-rate responses.
A pulse of seventy is not automatically reassuring in an unwell
patient taking one.

46
00:08:20,616 --> 00:08:30,864
Look at responsiveness, skin, pulse quality, breathing and pressure
relative to baseline. Ask about the actual last dose, not only the
medicine name.

47
00:08:30,864 --> 00:08:40,176
A medicine can contribute to a slow pulse or low pressure, but a
concurrent bleed, infection or cardiac problem may also be present.

48
00:08:40,176 --> 00:08:49,176
Avoid the shortcut that one mechanism explains everything. Use the
relevant clinical pathway and reassess after permitted care.

49
00:08:49,176 --> 00:09:00,384
Ask specifically about anticoagulants and antiplatelet medicines
rather than relying only on the phrase blood thinner. Different agents
have different effects and reversal plans.

50
00:09:00,384 --> 00:09:11,280
After a fall, seek direct observations of head contact, loss of
consciousness and subsequent change. A patient may not recall the
mechanism accurately.

51
00:09:11,280 --> 00:09:22,368
A small visible bruise does not measure the extent of internal injury.
Delayed confusion or reduced function requires assessment even when
the initial injury seemed minor.

52
00:09:22,368 --> 00:09:33,600
Do not give an unverified reversal treatment or force the patient to
walk to prove stability. Communicate the exact agent history and
uncertainty to the receiving team.

53
00:09:33,600 --> 00:09:45,144
Opioids, benzodiazepines and some antipsychotic or other medicines can
affect alertness and balance. Combinations and acute changes in
clearance may increase risk.

54
00:09:45,144 --> 00:09:53,928
Assess breathing effectiveness as well as saturation and rate. A
sleepy patient on oxygen may still be ventilating inadequately.

55
00:09:53,928 --> 00:10:06,048
Clarify what was actually taken and when. Preserve possible trauma,
infection, glucose or neurological causes rather than concluding from
the medicine list alone.

56
00:10:06,048 --> 00:10:16,248
Support the observed threat through the correct pathway. Medication
awareness is not permission for an empirical reversal drug or
independent chronic dose change.

57
00:10:16,248 --> 00:10:29,496
Diuretics can change volume and electrolytes, but not every class
lowers potassium. Combine the medicine history with actual losses,
breathing, perfusion and available measurements.

58
00:10:29,496 --> 00:10:40,296
Some diabetes medicines create recurrent low-glucose risk. A single
corrected value or meal is not sufficient to dismiss the underlying
medication problem.

59
00:10:40,296 --> 00:10:54,264
Anticholinergic effects may contribute retention, constipation, dry
mouth or confusion. Do not automatically label urinary symptoms
infection or use dry mouth alone as a fluid-bolus indication.

60
00:10:54,264 --> 00:11:04,608
Seek a coherent timeline of changes rather than collecting a list
without interpretation. Several mechanisms can coexist and require a
supported plan.

61
00:11:04,608 --> 00:11:14,592
Ask about prescriptions, over-the-counter medicines, supplements and
products from another person. Explain why the information matters
without blaming the patient.

62
00:11:14,592 --> 00:11:24,168
Verify the exact product and actual last intake when possible. A
dispensing label describes the intended plan, not proof of adherence.

63
00:11:24,168 --> 00:11:35,760
Ask about cost, side effects, access, swallowing and assistance when
relevant. Missed treatment can reflect a practical barrier rather than
deliberate refusal.

64
00:11:35,760 --> 00:11:47,712
Obtain permission before collecting relevant packaging or contacting
collateral. Do not search unrelated personal storage simply because a
textbook suggests gathering all medicines.

65
00:11:47,712 --> 00:12:00,096
Nancy uses the GEMS framework to organize geriatric, environmental,
medical and social considerations. It complements rather than replaces
immediate assessment of life threats.

66
00:12:00,096 --> 00:12:11,064
Environmental observations can reveal cold exposure, access barriers
or a fall mechanism. Describe what you see without assuming that a
cluttered home proves neglect.

67
00:12:11,064 --> 00:12:22,968
Medical context includes the current complaint, baseline and
medicines. Social context includes support for meals, mobility or
treatment and what the patient wishes to preserve.

68
00:12:22,968 --> 00:12:31,896
Use the information for a safe plan and appropriate referral. Do not
delay urgent conveyance to complete a detailed social inventory.

69
00:12:31,896 --> 00:12:41,472
Address the patient respectfully by the name they prefer. Avoid
infantilizing terms or speaking only to family while the patient is
present.

70
00:12:41,472 --> 00:12:51,264
Check that hearing aids and glasses are available and working when
needed, and reduce competing noise. Shouting may distort the message
instead of helping.

71
00:12:51,264 --> 00:13:02,424
Use short clear questions, allow time and obtain an interpreter or
other support when appropriate. A slow answer is not itself evidence
of incapacity.

72
00:13:02,424 --> 00:13:11,712
Check understanding by asking for an explanation in the patient's own
words. Do not confuse repeating a phrase with understanding the
particular decision.

73
00:13:11,712 --> 00:13:22,488
Older age, dementia, hearing loss or a care-home address does not
independently determine capacity. Assess the actual decision using
supported communication.

74
00:13:22,488 --> 00:13:33,096
An acute change may interfere with understanding or communication, but
it still requires evidence and the actual consent framework rather
than an age-based shortcut.

75
00:13:33,096 --> 00:13:43,752
A family member is not automatically entitled to override a capable
adult. Identify the appropriate decision-maker and authority when a
substitute is needed.

76
00:13:43,752 --> 00:13:56,232
Use clinical consultation and the established urgent-care process when
uncertainty affects safety. Record the explanation, responses and
reasoning rather than merely writing confused.

77
00:13:56,232 --> 00:14:06,744
Delirium commonly involves an acute or fluctuating change in attention
and awareness. The onset and course matter more than whether the
patient is agitated.

78
00:14:06,744 --> 00:14:15,480
A chronic dementia history gives a baseline to compare, not a reason
to dismiss new change. Both processes can occur together.

79
00:14:15,480 --> 00:14:28,896
Consider breathing, glucose, perfusion, infection, neurological
disease, pain, retention and medicine effects according to the
presentation. Do not stop after one plausible explanation.

80
00:14:28,896 --> 00:14:41,616
Treat urgent threats and investigate through the appropriate pathway.
Reversibility and recovery are variable; recognizing delirium does not
promise an immediate complete return to baseline.

81
00:14:41,616 --> 00:14:52,344
A patient may become unusually quiet rather than restless. New
withdrawal, poor attention or reduced movement can still signal
serious illness.

82
00:14:52,344 --> 00:15:03,288
Ask for the usual routine and directly observe current responses. A
caregiver's description of a quiet personality is not a substitute for
the patient's actual attention today.

83
00:15:03,288 --> 00:15:13,224
Repeat breathing, glucose, perfusion and relevant neurological
findings. Sedation, exhaustion and other medical causes remain
possible.

84
00:15:13,224 --> 00:15:23,184
Use calm orientation and support, but do not mistake reduced activity
after an intervention for improvement without checking function and
physiology.

85
00:15:23,184 --> 00:15:33,048
Ask what preceded the fall, whether it was witnessed and how long the
patient remained down. Separate a reported trip from an unexplained
collapse.

86
00:15:33,048 --> 00:15:44,280
Assess both the cause and the resulting injury. Infection, bleeding,
glucose disturbance or a rhythm problem can coexist with a fracture or
head injury.

87
00:15:44,280 --> 00:15:54,336
A new loss of mobility is important even with mild pain. Do not use
absence of deformity or a patient's wish to avoid inconvenience to
exclude injury.

88
00:15:54,336 --> 00:16:04,656
Choose a safe movement plan and appropriate spinal or limb protection.
Do not force standing or orthostatic testing in an unstable or injured
patient.

89
00:16:04,656 --> 00:16:13,176
A patient saying they can walk is useful but not sufficient. Current
clinical risk and observed ability must support the plan.

90
00:16:13,176 --> 00:16:24,624
Use the actual movement assessment before staged mobility. A serious
cardiac, respiratory or limb threat may require a nonambulatory method
rather than a trial walk.

91
00:16:24,624 --> 00:16:35,808
If more than minimal assistance would be needed or symptoms worsen,
stop and change the plan. Additional manual lifters are not
automatically safer than a suitable aid.

92
00:16:35,808 --> 00:16:47,856
Communicate the plan, protect attachments and reassess after each
meaningful stage. Do not turn a school practice sequence into a
mandatory mobility challenge for every patient.

93
00:16:47,856 --> 00:16:58,200
Some patients cannot safely or comfortably lie flat because of spinal
curvature, pain or breathing difficulty. Forcing them flat may worsen
the problem.

94
00:16:58,200 --> 00:17:06,216
Support the natural position and fill relevant gaps with suitable
padding, while maintaining the actual injury and airway plan.

95
00:17:06,216 --> 00:17:15,456
Padding is not a reason to ignore new neurological symptoms or to
build an unstable stack. Check secure support and equipment fit.

96
00:17:15,456 --> 00:17:25,104
Reassess breathing, pain, perfusion and neurological findings after
positioning or movement. Ask the patient whether the new position
helps.

97
00:17:25,104 --> 00:17:37,944
Unexplained injuries, fear around a caregiver or unmet basic needs may
raise concern. Poverty, illness, difficult housing or a pressure
injury alone does not prove abuse.

98
00:17:37,944 --> 00:17:47,712
Give the patient a safe chance to speak privately when feasible. Avoid
an accusatory confrontation that could increase danger or prevent
urgent care.

99
00:17:47,712 --> 00:18:00,528
Document location, appearance, observed behavior and the words/source
of relevant accounts. Do not date a bruise precisely from color or
label a person a perpetrator without evidence.

100
00:18:00,528 --> 00:18:13,536
Respond to immediate danger and use the actual service and British
Columbia safeguarding process, with capacity and consent considered.
Adult and child reporting rules are not interchangeable.

101
00:18:13,536 --> 00:18:22,368
A clear baseline statement can be more useful than simply writing
elderly with dementia. Explain the specific new difference.

102
00:18:22,368 --> 00:18:32,760
Attribute collateral and preserve uncertainty about an unwitnessed
fall, last medicine dose or onset. Do not fill the record with
invented negatives.

103
00:18:32,760 --> 00:18:41,904
Record why a position or movement plan was modified and what happened
afterward. Include any incomplete measurement and why it was limited.

104
00:18:41,904 --> 00:18:51,744
Hand the next team a usable trajectory. A single final normal number
does not communicate the patient's actual risk or the preceding
deterioration.

105
00:18:51,744 --> 00:19:00,960
This original patient usually manages ordinary daily tasks and
conversation. Today there is a new loss of attention and alertness.

106
00:19:00,960 --> 00:19:11,088
A fall occurred two days earlier, but no reliable witness can confirm
head contact. Keep that uncertainty rather than recording no head
injury.

107
00:19:11,088 --> 00:19:23,280
The reported medicines change risk: anticoagulation raises bleeding
concern and a beta blocker may blunt tachycardia. The exact last doses
remain unconfirmed.

108
00:19:23,280 --> 00:19:34,056
Do not choose between old age, a medicine effect and an injury before
assessment. Assess the current threats and obtain a useful timeline in
parallel.

109
00:19:34,056 --> 00:19:44,016
These values are assumed training observations, not real patient data.
The normal-range pulse and glucose do not explain away the acute
functional change.

110
00:19:44,016 --> 00:19:56,496
Assess airway protection and breathing effectiveness, then perfusion
and focused neurological findings. The rate of twenty-four and altered
response deserve context and reassessment.

111
00:19:56,496 --> 00:20:07,944
The absence of fever does not exclude infection, and the medicine
history prevents relying on pulse alone. The unwitnessed head-contact
history also remains relevant.

112
00:20:07,944 --> 00:20:19,056
Do not administer fluid solely because pressure is lower than a
reported usual value. Assess the full mechanism and current pathway
while arranging appropriate urgent care.

113
00:20:19,056 --> 00:20:29,424
Ask for the last confirmed usual conversation and whether function
changed immediately after the fall or later. Distinguish observed time
from an estimate.

114
00:20:29,424 --> 00:20:42,600
Ask about new pain, vomiting, headache, weakness or speech changes
without assuming they must be present. Seek a focused examination
rather than forcing painful movement.

115
00:20:42,600 --> 00:20:53,208
Clarify last doses and recent changes with permission, using reliable
packaging or collateral. Do not infer adherence or overdose from a
medicine list.

116
00:20:53,208 --> 00:21:03,120
Keep infection, metabolic and cardiac alternatives visible. A
suspected head injury should not prevent recognition of a second
concurrent emergency.

117
00:21:03,120 --> 00:21:12,720
Assume a new left-arm weakness is now observed. State precisely what
changed rather than inserting a final diagnosis into the case.

118
00:21:12,720 --> 00:21:25,152
Repeat focused findings and primary threats, and use the applicable
stroke or trauma pathway with the full history. The field examination
cannot reliably exclude intracranial bleeding.

119
00:21:25,152 --> 00:21:34,680
Preserve the distinct times and uncertainties. The time a deficit is
first noticed is not automatically the onset or the last-known-well
time.

120
00:21:34,680 --> 00:21:46,416
Pre-alert with the neurological change, anticoagulant history, recent
fall and current physiology. Do not delay for an unnecessary walk test
or a perfect medication inventory.

121
00:21:46,416 --> 00:21:56,808
Select a position that supports airway and breathing while respecting
the actual injury plan. Avoid forcing kyphotic anatomy into an unsafe
flat posture.

122
00:21:56,808 --> 00:22:05,640
Use the safe movement method justified by current risk and ability. Do
not ask the patient to walk simply to save equipment or time.

123
00:22:05,640 --> 00:22:15,456
Protect skin and attachments, cover appropriately and keep
communication direct. An urgent plan can still preserve privacy and
explanation.

124
00:22:15,456 --> 00:22:26,112
Continue checking response during transfer and conveyance. Escalation
is not the end of care and a first normal monitor result does not
close reassessment.

125
00:22:26,112 --> 00:22:34,896
Begin with the meaningful change from baseline and the current
concern. Give relevant times and the reliability of each source.

126
00:22:34,896 --> 00:22:44,424
Report medicines and injury facts without overstating certainty. Use
unknown rather than a fabricated no when information is absent.

127
00:22:44,424 --> 00:22:54,648
Describe the actual neurological and physiological trend and the
response to care. A label such as elderly fall or possible UTI is not
enough.

128
00:22:54,648 --> 00:23:05,904
Explain movement modifications and remaining threats. The receiving
team needs a reliable clinical story, not a collection of reassuring
isolated numbers.

129
00:23:05,904 --> 00:23:16,560
You should be able to recognize new illness even when pain, fever or
tachycardia is absent. That requires a real baseline and a complete
primary assessment.

130
00:23:16,560 --> 00:23:25,032
Explain how medicines and reserve alter the presentation without
making the drug list or the patient's age the final diagnosis.

131
00:23:25,032 --> 00:23:34,584
Demonstrate supported communication and safe movement using the actual
tools and authorization. Preserve dignity as part of clinical quality.

132
00:23:34,584 --> 00:23:43,848
Then reassess and report the trajectory. The same baseline-change-
modification framework will guide the next session on larger-bodied
patients.
